# The approval body reserves the lines it draws, so no flag is cut off.
#
# The card measured its height with wrapped_line_count(), which leaves an
# over-long word on a line of its own, and drew with the span pass, which
# breaks it. A command line is nothing but over-long words, so at 340x620 the
# fixture reserved 8 rows for a command that wraps to 20: render_ask_wrapped()
# ellipsised at row 8 and the last twelve rows were never built.
#
# What that hid was the whole tail of the command --- --out=, --notify=,
# --skip-reconcile-check, and --allow-outside-workspace, the flag this very
# approval exists to ask about. The head still said "bash may write outside
# the workspace" while the only evidence of it was off the card.
#
# settings: {"window_width":340,"window_height":620,"open_tabs":["t2"],"active_tab":"t2","theme":"dark"}
# env: HANABI_ASK_DEMO=longapproval HANABI_WIN_W=340 HANABI_WIN_H=620

# Break points re-pinned at afterhours 1ac6db2: upstream 82145f9 measures text
# by advance instead of the ink box, so the same column packs one more
# glyph per row (16 rather than 15) and the command is 19 rows, not 20.
# Inspected at 340x620: every row whole, nothing clipped.
require_thread t2
expect_text "The agent needs approval  ·  bash"
expect_text "bash may write"

# Twenty rows reserved, twenty rows built. Under the old measure the row list
# stopped at eight and this element did not exist, so this line times out on a
# revert.
# Every flag present and in order -- the whole command reassembles from the
# rows, whatever the face made of the break points.
# The rows draw the approval INPUT as journaled -- the command inside its
# JSON object -- so that is the text they must reassemble to.
expect_ui_rows_join ask_approval_input '{"command":"./scripts/release_batch.sh --force --cycle=2026-09 --ledger=/var/finance/payouts/2026-09/ledger.csv --out=/var/finance/payouts/2026-09/settled.csv --notify=payout-owner@example.test --skip-reconcile-check --allow-outside-workspace"}'

# Nothing was cut to make it fit. The pre-fix build ended its last row with an
# ellipsis right here, and that ellipsis was the whole defect: it is what a
# reader saw instead of the flag.
expect_no_text "ledger.csv…"
expect_no_text "settled.csv…"

# The content is taller than the viewport, which is the honest state: the tail
# is off-screen and reachable, not absent. (a_long_approval_scrolls_to_its_
# last_flag.e2e walks it into view.)
# The tail is below the fold: the last flag's row exists but is not painted
# in the viewport (the scroll script walks it into view).
# A flag from the middle of the command is painted (named by a token no wrap
# can split -- the flag word itself, not a Roboto-era fragment of a path)
# while the last flag is not.
expect_text "--ledger="
expect_no_text "--allow-outside-"
